comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-03T06:34:01.241Z","harness:claude-code","Refinement from a live repair on 2026-08-03, narrowing this finding rather than restating it.\n\nI reproduced the duplicate-row condition by accident (adding related pm-q91qyd to pm-ki67py, which already carried that exact edge) and then repaired it. The repair did not require destroying both copies: --dep-remove accepts id=<id>,kind=<value>,source_kind=<value>, and the two rows differed in source_kind (cli:create:dep vs cli:update:dep), so the newer one was removable on its own. The audit went from 12 findings back to the 11-finding baseline with the original edge intact and no interval during which the relationship was absent.\n\nSo the destructive-remediation problem is real but narrower than stated: it applies to rows that are identical in all three selector dimensions, not to every duplicate the audit reports. The audit's own sample line already prints the pair without the discriminator, which is why the remediation reads as all-or-nothing - 'pm-ki67py -> pm-q91qyd (related x2)' does not say the two rows are separable.\n\nTwo consequences for this item's scope. First, the sample should print the discriminating fields when they differ, so the reader can see whether a precise removal exists. Second, the genuinely unrepairable case is duplicate rows agreeing on id, kind and source_kind, and that is the population worth counting - the current count conflates it with the separable case."
  "2026-08-07T05:38:47.834Z","harness:claude-code","Recurrence confirmed 2026-08-07, and the class is now ratcheted.\n\nThe finding returned. Three exactly duplicated rows were present on active holders: pm-cg1sjb toward pm-ei6x66 and toward pm-zlv8jl, and pm-ei6x66 toward pm-rtn5h6, all kind related. Every duplicate carries source_kind cli:update:dep with created_at on 2026-08-05, so they were minted by dependency-appending mutations during an enrichment pass over items that already held the same edge. The original reproduction on 2026-07-28 used pm-dkrmzv and a different kind, so this is a second independent occurrence of the same write-time behaviour rather than surviving damage.\n\nThe destructive repair reproduced exactly as described. Removing with the most precise selector the flag accepts deleted both copies and left the holder with no edge toward the target, verified by reading the dependency list immediately afterwards. Restoring required a second mutation, so each of the three repairs passed through a state where a real relationship did not exist, and the history now records three removals and three additions rather than three deduplications. Six mutations to collapse three duplicates.\n\nThe class is now enforced. A graph_finding selector source was added to the tracker measurement ratchet and duplicate_dependency_row is declared with a ceiling of zero, owned by this item, so a fresh duplicate fails the gate before merge instead of waiting for a reviewer to read the audit. The source is exhaustive, so an undeclared audit finding code also surfaces. This does not substitute for the acceptance criteria here: the ratchet catches the row after it is written, and the criteria ask for the write to be idempotent or for an occurrence-scoped remediation to exist. It only removes the silence between the write and the discovery.\n"
  "2026-08-20T17:35:06.903Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence 2026-08-20: adding an exact dependency identity now normalizes every matching legacy row to one row atomically, preserving the first recorded row and never passing through an absent-edge state. Repeating the same add is safely idempotent when only one row exists. A temporary end-to-end tracker started with an exact duplicate, reapplied the identity once, and graph audit moved duplicate_dependency_row from one to zero while the relationship remained present exactly once. Live repository audit also reports zero duplicate_dependency_row findings. Linked mutation and audit tests passed 29/29."
  "2026-08-20T17:37:46.201Z","harness:codex","Repository-wide verification 2026-08-20: pnpm test:coverage passed 539 files and 7,644 tests at exact 100% statements, branches, functions, and lines; pnpm quality:static passed generated contracts, SDK surface, package parity, grammar, token budgets, tracker context, graph composition, record integrity, and transport floors; ESLint passed with zero warnings; jscpd found zero clones across 1,067 files; docs links passed; secret scan found no credential-like material; pnpm audit found no known vulnerabilities. Manual acceptance used a disposable initialized tracker and verified semantic traversal, duplicate collapse without edge absence, malformed removal refusal, and typed zero-match recovery."
  "2026-08-20T17:55:36.526Z","harness:codex","Hosted review remediation: classified repeated exact dependency rows as a production_defect and recorded strengthened gate evidence covering one-row survival, no absent-edge interval, idempotent touch-normalization, and zero duplicate audit findings."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-20T17:35:07.554Z","harness:codex","Touch-normalization is the safe bridge between write-time idempotence and legacy repair: normalize only the exact identity named by the mutation, preserve one provenance row, and leave unrelated relationships untouched."
